      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Autonomous agents, defined as intelligent entities that autonomously perform specific tasks through\nenvironmental perception, strategic self-planning, and action execution [6, 36, 98], have emerged\nas a rapidly expanding research field since the 1990s [93]. Despite initial advancements, these early\niterations often lack the sophistication of human intelligence [127]. However, the recent advent\nof Large Language Models (LLMs) [65] has marked a turning point. This LLM breakthrough has\ndemonstrated cognitive abilities nearing human levels in planning and reasoning [3, 65], which\naligns with the e",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The opening of the introduction, with the authors' addresses and the ACM permission notice. Autonomous agents, entities that perceive their environment, plan and act, have been studied since the 1990s, but early ones lacked human-like sophistication; LLMs, with planning and reasoning the authors describe as nearing human level, have renewed research interest.",
