      "narrative": null,
      "content": "them as LLM-based agents in this paper).\nNevertheless, the application of singular LLM-based agents encounters limitations, since real-\nworld problems often span multiple domains, requiring expertise from various fields. In response\nto this challenge, developing LLM-Based Multi-Agent (LMA) systems represents a pivotal evolution,\naiming to boost performance via synergistic collaboration. An LMA system harnesses the strengths\nof multiple specialized agents, each with unique skills and responsibilities. These agents work in\nconcert towards a common goal, engaging in collaborative activities like",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The rest of the introduction. Single LLM-based agents are limited because real problems span domains, so LMA systems combine specialised agents that collaborate through debate and discussion, which prior work links to divergent thinking, better factuality and reasoning, and thorough validation. For software engineering the authors name three benefits: autonomous problem-solving by splitting requirements into sub-tasks, mirroring agile practice; robustness and fault tolerance through cross-examination, which counters hallucination much as code review and testing do; and scalability, by adding agents and reallocating tasks as projects grow. The article reviews LMA applications, runs two case studies and proposes a two-phase agenda: enhancing individual agents, then optimising their collaboration.",
