      "narrative": null,
      "content": "(3) Quality Assurance: bug, fault, defect, fuzz, test, vulnerab, verificat, validat\n(4) Maintenance: debug, repair, review, refactor, patch, maintenanc\nWe focus on four key phases of the SDLC: requirements engineering, code generation, quality\nassurance, and software maintenance. For each phase, the relevant SE keywords are combined\nusing the OR operator to capture all variations. The final search query for each SDLC phase follows\nthe format: [agent words] AND [SE words].\nFollowing the guide of previous work [99, 128, 168], we design the following inclusion and\nexclusion criteria. In the first",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Selection criteria, snowballing, requirements engineering and code generation. SE keywords cover four phases (requirements, code generation, quality assurance, maintenance); criteria exclude short papers, duplicates, non-research items, surveys, non-LMA or single-agent work, papers before ChatGPT's November 2022 release, non-SE work and papers without experiments, leaving 41 studies on 14 November 2024, with backward and forward snowballing adding 30 more. In requirements engineering, Elicitron simulates diverse users for elicitation, MARE uses five agents across elicitation to specification, and Sami et al. use four agents to generate and prioritise user stories. In code generation the common roles are Orchestrator (PairCoder's Navigator, Self-Organized Agents, CODES's RepoSketcher), Programmer with Reviewer and Tester in iterative feedback loops (INTERVENOR, Self-repair, TGen), and Information Retriever (Agent4PLC, MapCoder, CodexGraph); Agent Forest instead samples many outputs and picks the one most similar to the others.",
