      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Software Quality Assurance",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-33-software-quality-assurance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In this subsection, we review related work on testing, vulnerability detection, bug detection, and\nfault localization, with a focus on how LMA systems are being employed to enhance software\nquality assurance processes.\nTesting. Fuzz4All [149] generates testing input for software systems across multiple programming\nlanguages. In this framework, a distillation agent reduces user input while a generation agent\ncreates and mutates inputs. AXNav [123] is designed to automate accessibility testing. It interprets\nnatural language test instructions and executes accessibility tests, such as VoiceOver,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Software quality assurance. Testing: Fuzz4All (distillation and generation agents for fuzzing inputs), AXNav (planner, action and evaluation agents for iOS accessibility tests), WhiteFox (compiler-optimisation fuzzing), plus penetration, user acceptance and GUI testing. Vulnerability detection: GPTLens (auditor agents with a critic that ranks findings and filters false positives), MuCoLD (tester and developer roles reaching consensus) and cross-validation of several LLMs' answers. Bug detection: ICAA, with tool-using agents, a false-positive pruner and code-intention consistency checking. Fault localisation: RCAgent for cloud root-cause analysis and AgentFL's comprehension, navigation and confirmation agents.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
