      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-34-software-maintenance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In this subsection, we explore related work on debugging and code review, highlighting how LMA\nsystems contribute to automating and improving software maintenance processes.\nDebugging. Debugging involves identifying, locating, and resolving software bugs. Several frame-\nworks, including MASAI [8], MarsCode [86], AutoSD [64], and others [19, 73, 92, 125], follow a\nstructured process consisting of stages like bug reproduction, fault localization, patch generation,\nand validation. Specialized agents are typically responsible for each stage. FixAgent [70] includes a\ndebugging agent and a program r",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Software maintenance (with Figure 1, contrasting Waterfall and Agile multi-agent development). Debugging frameworks such as MASAI, MarsCode and AutoSD stage the work into reproduction, fault localisation, patch generation and validation with a specialised agent per stage; others include FixAgent, the quiz-based MASTER, AutoCodeRover with spectrum-based localisation, SpecRover, ACFIX for access-control vulnerabilities in smart contracts, DEI for re-ranking patches across agents, SWE-Search with Monte Carlo tree search and a debating discriminator, and RepoUnderstander's repository knowledge graph. Code review systems include Rasheed et al.'s four-agent reviewer and CodeAgent with its QA-Checker supervisor; Lemner et al. predict which test cases need maintenance after code changes.",
        "state": "draft",
