    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-2404.04834v4/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "End-to-end Software Development",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-35-end-to-end-software-development.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "End-to-end software development encompasses the entire process of creating a software product.\nWhile conventional code generation is often limited to producing isolated components such as\nfunctions, classes, or modules, end-to-end development starts from high-level software requirements\nand progresses through design, implementation, testing, and ultimately delivering a fully functional\nand ready-to-use product.\nIn practice, developers and stakeholders typically adopt established software process models to\nguide collaboration, such as Agile [26] and Waterfall [105]. Similarly, the design of LMA",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "End-to-end software development. Unlike code generation of isolated components, end-to-end work goes from requirements to a delivered product, and LMA designs borrow software process models (FlowGen and Self-Collaboration are excluded because they only generate code segments). Waterfall-style systems such as MetaGPT chain product manager, architect, engineer and QA roles; AgileCoder and AgileGen use Agile roles and sprints, AgileGen adding user involvement and Gherkin requirements. Think-on-Process and MegaAgent generate process instances or agent roles dynamically from the requirements, and Co-Learning and iterative experience refinement reuse experience from earlier projects.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-24"
